---
import {
  Analytics01Icon,
  CloudIcon,
  ComputerTerminal01Icon,
  McpServerIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'Hosted MCP', text: 'Connect seoskill.dev/mcp to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or any remote MCP client.', href: '/docs/mcp', icon: McpServerIcon },
  { title: 'CLI and local MCP', text: 'Install seo for the site crawl, internal links and Bing.', href: '/docs/cli', icon: ComputerTerminal01Icon },
  { title: 'Run in cloud agents', text: 'Set up the same tools for a cloud agent or CI job that starts on a fresh machine each run.', href: '/docs/cloud-agents', icon: CloudIcon },
  { title: 'seo report', text: 'The Search Console report your agent reads first.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Analytics01Icon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="AI SEO agent with your own search data"
  description="Turn Claude Code, Codex or Cursor into an AI SEO agent that reads your Search Console, Google Analytics and keyword data. Free MCP server, CLI and skill for SEO automation."
  path="/features/seo-agent"
  lead="The coding agent you already use can be your SEO agent. Connect SEO Skill and it reads your clicks, queries, index status and crawl before it suggests a change."
  {related}
>
  <h2>What your SEO agent can do with SEO Skill</h2>
  <p>
    SEO Skill fetches your data and returns facts with their dates and
    limits. It gives no advice. Your agent reads those facts, explains what
    changed, and points to the pages and queries behind it. If the agent
    also works in your site's code, it can make the change you agree on and
    then check the page again.
  </p>
  <p>
    You still decide what ships. The agent shows its evidence first, so you
    can see why it wants to touch a page before anything changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Connect the hosted MCP or install the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    The hosted MCP needs nothing installed. Add it to your agent and sign in
    with Google when it asks. For Claude Code:
  </p>
  <pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http --scope user seoskill https://seoskill.dev/mcp</code></pre>
  <p>For Codex:</p>
  <pre><code>codex mcp add seoskill --url https://seoskill.dev/mcp</code></pre>
  <p>
    The site crawl, internal links and Bing Webmaster Tools run on your own
    machine. For those, install the CLI with <code>npm i -g seo</code> and
    run <code>seo start</code>. Setup can add the skill to your agents (and
    if you skip that, <code>seo skill install</code> adds it later).
  </p>

  <h2>Jobs to give your SEO agent</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ask</th><th>What the agent reads</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What changed in search last week?</td><td>Clicks, impressions, click-through rate and position against the period before, with the pages and queries that moved.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which titles should I rewrite?</td><td>Queries in positions 1 to 4 with a click-through rate below your site's own curve, and the page that ranks for each.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Why did this page stop getting clicks?</td><td>The live page, its Google index status and its queries against the period before.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which pages are not indexed, and why?</td><td>Google's own indexing reasons for up to 100 known URLs a run, each checked live.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Did last week's release hurt search?</td><td>Equal periods before and after the release date, with any Google updates in the window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What do competitors rank for that we don't?</td><td>Ranked keywords and SERPs from your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Automate SEO checks with a scheduled agent</h2>
  <p>
    SEO automation here means your agent runs the same checks on a schedule
    and tells you what moved. A weekly task that runs the Search Console
    report, opens the section that changed most and lists the pages to look
    at is a good start. The <a href="/docs/cloud-agents">cloud agents
    guide</a> shows how to give an agent that starts fresh each time access
    to your account.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report can also keep a list of the issues it found for a site, so
    the next session starts where the last one stopped. Your agent marks
    each issue fixed or ignored as you decide. The hosted server keeps that
    list for sites in the weekly site audit. For any other site, your agent
    saves the list and passes it back next time.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you would rather we watch the site for you, the weekly site audit
    crawls it every week and checks Google's index every day on our
    servers, with alerts when a page drops out. It costs $50 a month per
    site. The <a href="/features/seo-monitoring">SEO monitoring</a> page
    explains what it covers.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep the agent honest about cause</h2>
  <p>
    A Google update in the same week as a drop shows that the timing
    matched. It does not prove the update caused it.
    A <code>noindex</code> tag is reported as what the page says, and it is only a problem if you
    did not mean it. Ask your agent to show the evidence for a claim before
    it rewrites twenty title tags on a hunch.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
